<Chapter id="handoff">
<h2>Starting Sunken</h2>
  <p>Front authenticates the Battle.net account and selects an SC2 game account.
  It then supplies the data for the Sunken connection.</p>
  <p>The <code>logon_response</code> attribute contains a bit-packed
  <code>Battlenet::Client::Authentication::LogonResponse3</code>. This type contains
  the status, authentication requests, timeouts, regulator rules, account data,
  and optional RAF state.</p>
  <p>The selected BSN metadata type determines the protobuf blob's logical bit length. All unused
  bits in the last byte must be zero.</p>
  <p>The <code>address</code> attribute contains the Sunken host and optional port.
  Port 1119 is used when the string omits it. A bracketed IPv6 address can
  include a port after the closing bracket.</p>
  <p>The client can open the Sunken TCP connection before Front closes. The client
  must send <a href="#resume-name"><code>Auth/1 ResumeRequest</code></a> after Front
  closes. Front and Sunken are independent TCP connections.</p>
  <p>The Front protocol defines <code>RequestDisconnect</code> method 7 and
  <code>ForceDisconnect</code> method 4. The documented handoff sends a WebSocket
  close frame directly.</p>
  <p>The Sunken TCP connection opens first, followed by the Front close.
  <code>Auth/1 ResumeRequest</code> is sent after the close call succeeds.</p>
  <p>After the handoff, the client uses the Sunken transport and payload format.
  The Sunken record section defines its bit cursor, header, and boundary.</p>
